  "series": "LINUX PERMISSIONS: A FIELD HANDBOOK",
  "date": "29/08/2026",
  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "The Unix Security Model",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Identities, One Bridge",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Linux is multi-user by design, always.",
            "Every process runs on behalf of a user.",
            "Every file belongs to one user, one group.",
            "Permissions are the bridge between the two."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Kernel Sees Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Users have a numeric UID, groups a GID.",
            "/etc/passwd and /etc/group are only labels.",
            "The kernel compares integers, never names.",
            "UID 0 = root: nearly all checks skipped.",
            "Root's bypass is CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE, not rwx."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "id -u alice          # 1000  numeric UID",
            "id alice             # uid, gid, groups",
            "getent passwd 1000   # number -> label"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Process Carries Three UIDs"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "UID",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "real",
              "who launched it"
            ],
            [
              "effective",
              "used for perm checks"
            ],
            [
              "saved",
              "what it may switch back to"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What The Inode Stores"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Owner UID, group GID, 16-bit mode field.",
            "That is all of access control. Nothing else.",
            "Mode = 4 special bits + 9 permission bits.",
            "9 bits = 3 classes x rwx."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "setuid setgid sticky",
              "user rwx",
              "group rwx",
              "other rwx"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "DAC Under MAC"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "rwx bits are Discretionary: the owner decides.",
            "SELinux / AppArmor add Mandatory control on top.",
            "MAC can deny what DAC happily allows.",
            "It never grants what DAC already denied."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Checked At open(), Not read()",
          "items": [
            "The check runs once, when the fd is created.",
            "chmod after that does not close open fds.",
            "A running process keeps its old access."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Decoding ls -l"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "-rw-r--r-- 1 alice devs 4096 Jan 3 f.txt",
            "# type | 9 bits | links | owner | group",
            "#      | size | mtime | name"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Char",
            "Type"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "- / d",
              "regular / directory"
            ],
            [
              "l / s",
              "symlink / socket"
            ],
            [
              "c / b",
              "char dev / block dev"
            ],
            [
              "p",
              "FIFO (named pipe)"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Unix Security Model (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Symlink Modes Are Fiction",
          "text": "ls -l shows lrwxrwxrwx for every symlink. A link's own bits are meaningless — the target's mode governs access."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "First Matching Class Wins",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Order is strict: user, then group, then other.",
            "The first class you match decides everything.",
            "Later classes are never consulted at all.",
            "There is no falling through to a wider class."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "chmod 077 Locks You Out",
          "text": "As owner you match the user class first — 000 for you. Everyone else gets rwx. Being the owner is not a shortcut past your own bits."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Each Bit Buys"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Bit",
            "Val",
            "On a file"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "r",
              "4",
              "read contents, cat, cp source"
            ],
            [
              "w",
              "2",
              "truncate, append, overwrite"
            ],
            [
              "x",
              "1",
              "kernel may exec() it"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "w never permits delete or rename.",
            "Those live on the parent directory.",
            "x is required for binaries AND scripts."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Scripts Need r And x"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The interpreter must read the script text.",
            "A compiled binary needs only x, never r.",
            "So scripts want 755 or 700, not 711."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chmod 644 s.sh",
            "./s.sh        # Permission denied  (no x)",
            "bash s.sh     # works: bash only reads it"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "The Tightening Trap",
          "items": [
            "Devs test with `bash script.sh` and see green.",
            "Cron calls ./script.sh and dies on exec.",
            "Same file, same mode, different failure."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How Group Class Matches"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Match on primary OR any supplementary group.",
            "`id` prints every group you actually carry.",
            "Groups are snapshotted at login time.",
            "usermod -aG needs a new session to bite.",
            "`newgrp devs` starts a shell with it now."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "sudo usermod -aG devs alice",
            "id -nG alice   # still old set in this shell",
            "newgrp devs    # or log out and back in"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Decode Drill"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Field",
            "Bits",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "user",
              "rwx",
              "read, write, execute"
            ],
            [
              "group",
              "r-x",
              "read, execute"
            ],
            [
              "other",
              "---",
              "nothing at all"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Display order is fixed: rwxrwxrwx, never shuffled.",
            "A `-` simply means that bit is off.",
            "-rwxr-x--- is the row above, in one string."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Directory Is A Table",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "It maps names to inode numbers. Nothing more.",
            "rwx applies to that table, not to file contents.",
            "So dir bits gate names; file bits gate data."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Nine Bits on Files",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Three Bits, Remapped"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Bit",
            "On a directory"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "r",
              "list the names inside (ls)"
            ],
            [
              "x",
              "traverse: resolve a name to an inode"
            ],
            [
              "w",
              "create, delete, rename entries"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "x Is The Important One",
          "text": "Traversal is what reaches a file at all. r only reveals the name list; without x the name is useless to you."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Deletion Lives On The Parent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Removing a file needs w+x on the PARENT dir.",
            "The file's own mode is irrelevant to unlink.",
            "You can delete a file you cannot read.",
            "You cannot delete a file you own in a locked dir."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "r Without x"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chmod 400 dir",
            "ls -l dir     # names print, stat fails",
            "# ?????????  ? ? ? ?  ? secret.txt"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "x Without r: Dark Dirs"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chmod 100 dir",
            "ls dir              # Permission denied",
            "cat dir/secret.txt  # works if name known"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Contents unlistable, exact paths still reachable.",
            "Classic obscurity pattern for /home layouts.",
            "Obscurity, not security — names leak elsewhere."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Every Component Needs x"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Reaching /a/b/c.txt needs x on /, /a and /a/b.",
            "One missing x anywhere breaks the whole path.",
            "The error names the file; the cause is upstream."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "chmod 700 ~ Breaks nginx",
          "items": [
            "The daemon can no longer traverse into your home.",
            "So ~/public_html is unreachable, mode notwithstanding.",
            "Homes usually want 711 or 750 with the right group."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pair r And x"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Directories want 755 or 750 almost always.",
            "r without x is a nearly useless directory.",
            "700 for private trees, 750 for team trees."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Directories: x Means Search",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Recursive Catastrophe"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chmod -R 644 dir   # strips x: tree bricked",
            "chmod -R a+rX dir  # X = x only where useful",
            "# capital X: dirs, or files already exec"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Each Triad Is 3 Bits",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "r = 4, w = 2, x = 1. Sum them.",
            "Every digit lands in 0-7.",
            "Three digits = user, group, other."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "N",
            "Bits",
            "N",
            "Bits"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0",
              "---",
              "4",
              "r--"
            ],
            [
              "1",
              "--x",
              "5",
              "r-x"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "-w-",
              "6",
              "rw-"
            ],
            [
              "3",
              "-wx",
              "7",
              "rwx"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Fourth Digit"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Leading digit holds the special bits.",
            "setuid = 4, setgid = 2, sticky = 1.",
            "4755 = setuid plus rwxr-xr-x.",
            "Omitted, it is treated as 0."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "0755 Is Not 755",
          "text": "Writing the leading zero explicitly CLEARS any special bits already set. That is how a setgid project directory quietly loses its inheritance."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "File Modes To Memorise"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mode",
            "Bits",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "644",
              "rw-r--r--",
              "normal file"
            ],
            [
              "600",
              "rw-------",
              "private file"
            ],
            [
              "664",
              "rw-rw-r--",
              "group-writable"
            ],
            [
              "400",
              "r--------",
              "read-only secret"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Directory Modes"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mode",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "755",
              "standard directory"
            ],
            [
              "750",
              "group-private"
            ],
            [
              "700",
              "fully private"
            ],
            [
              "1777",
              "world-writable + sticky (/tmp)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Script And Binary Modes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "755 — executable by everyone.",
            "700 — private script, owner only.",
            "4755 — setuid binary. Audit every one."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Conversion Drill"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "rw-r-----  ->  4+2=6 | 4 | 0  ->  640",
            "2775 -> setgid, no sticky",
            "     -> rwx | rwx | r-x  (shared project)"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Octal Notation",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Verify In One Command"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "stat -c '%a %A %U %G' f",
            "# 640 -rw-r----- alice devs",
            "# octal, symbolic, owner, group at once"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Syntaxes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Absolute octal sets every bit at once: chmod 644 f.",
            "Symbolic edits bits relative to current state.",
            "Octal for known targets, symbolic for tweaks."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Symbolic Grammar"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Slot",
            "Values"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "who",
              "u g o a"
            ],
            [
              "op",
              "+ - ="
            ],
            [
              "perm",
              "r w x X s t"
            ],
            [
              "copy",
              "u g o as source"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "+ adds bits, - removes bits.",
            "= sets exactly, clearing unlisted bits in that class.",
            "chmod g=r f leaves group with read only."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Bare + Respects umask"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chmod +w f    # masked by your umask",
            "chmod a+w f   # ignores umask, truly all",
            "# same intent, different result. Be explicit."
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Clauses And Copies"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chmod u=rwx,g=rx,o= dir   # equals 750",
            "chmod g=u file            # group copies user",
            "chmod --reference=good.conf new.conf"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Comma clauses apply left to right, in order.",
            "--reference copies a known-good file's mode."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Capital X, The Safe Recursion"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "X sets x only on directories.",
            "Or on files that already have some x bit.",
            "This is the tool for whole trees."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chmod -R u+rwX,go+rX,go-w dir",
            "# readable + traversable by all,",
            "# writable only by me. The canonical recipe."
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Who May chmod"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Only the file's owner, or root. Full stop.",
            "Being in the file's group is not enough.",
            "Even w on the file does not let you chmod it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Symlinks Are Not Chmod-able",
          "text": "Linux has no settable mode on a symlink. chmod resolves through the link and changes the target instead."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Audit Sweeps"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "find /usr/local -perm -o+w -type f",
            "find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null",
            "# world-writable files, then every setuid"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "chmod: Changing Modes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Four Forms",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Command",
            "Changes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "chown u f",
              "owner only"
            ],
            [
              "chown u:g f",
              "owner and group"
            ],
            [
              "chown :g f",
              "group only"
            ],
            [
              "chgrp g f",
              "group (dedicated form)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Only Root Gives Files Away"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A user cannot hand a file to another user.",
            "Otherwise disk quotas would be trivially dodged.",
            "And you could plant files as someone else.",
            "A non-root owner may change group — to their own groups."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "chown Strips setuid/setgid",
          "text": "When a non-root user chowns a file the kernel silently clears 4000 and 2000. That kills the 'hand someone a setuid shell' attack."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Flags Worth Knowing"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "-R recurses the whole tree.",
            "-h acts on the symlink itself, not its target.",
            "--from=old:oldgrp scopes the change safely.",
            "--reference=template copies another file's ownership."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chown -R --from=root:root app:app /srv/app",
            "chown --reference=template new.conf",
            "# scoped and copied beats blind recursion"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Typos Here Are Unrecoverable",
          "items": [
            "chown -R user:group / rewrites the whole system.",
            "There is no undo; only a reinstall or backup.",
            "Echo the path before you run the command."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Numbers Work Directly"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chown 1000:1000 file   # no /etc/passwd needed",
            "tar --numeric-owner -cf a.tar dir",
            "# containers and foreign mounts: use numbers"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Names may not exist on the target system.",
            "Tarballs and NFS preserve numbers, not names.",
            "UID 1000 here may be a different person there."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "chown and chgrp",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Copying Preserves"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Op",
            "Owner",
            "Mode"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "cp",
              "copier",
              "umask-filtered"
            ],
            [
              "cp -p / -a",
              "as root, kept",
              "kept"
            ],
            [
              "mv (same fs)",
              "kept",
              "kept, same inode"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Production Classic"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Deploy runs as root; files land owned by root.",
            "The service runs as its own user and cannot write.",
            "Result: 'permission denied' at 3am, every time.",
            "Fix: chown -R svc:svc after deploy, or deploy as svc."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Mask Of Bits To Remove",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Per-process. Applies when a program creates a file.",
            "It clears bits from what the program requested.",
            "It can never add a permission, only take one away."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "It Is AND NOT, Not Minus"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "final = requested AND NOT umask",
            "# 666 - umask happens to work for 022, 027",
            "# it breaks for odd masks. Use the bit view."
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Base Modes Programs Ask For"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Regular files: 0666. Directories: 0777.",
            "The kernel never sets x on a new regular file.",
            "That is why umask 022 gives 644 and 755."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Three Masks You Meet"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "umask",
            "Files",
            "Dirs",
            "Who loses"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "022",
              "644",
              "755",
              "g,o lose w"
            ],
            [
              "002",
              "664",
              "775",
              "o loses w"
            ],
            [
              "077",
              "600",
              "700",
              "g,o lose all"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "022 is the distro default on most systems.",
            "002 pairs with setgid shared-group directories.",
            "077 is the hardened choice for secrets work."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "umask: The Creation Filter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reading And Setting It"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "umask          # 0027  current octal mask",
            "umask -S       # u=rwx,g=rx,o=  symbolic",
            "umask 027      # this shell + its children"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where It Persists"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Inherited by children; not saved anywhere by itself.",
            "/etc/profile or ~/.bashrc for login shells.",
            "UMask= in a systemd unit for services.",
            "UMASK in /etc/login.defs for the system default."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Two Traps",
          "items": [
            "Creation-time only: existing files never change.",
            "A wrong tree needs chmod, not a new umask.",
            "If a program requests 0600, umask 000 changes nothing."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Tools That Bypass It"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "install sets modes explicitly with -m.",
            "cp -p and tar -x as root restore stored modes.",
            "git checkout honours only its own exec bit."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Worked Case: umask 027"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "umask 027",
            "touch f   # 0666 & ~027 -> 640",
            "mkdir d   # 0777 & ~027 -> 750",
            "# open(...,0666) still lands on 640"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Borrowed Privilege",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Bit",
            "Octal",
            "At exec time"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "setuid",
              "4000",
              "euid = file owner"
            ],
            [
              "setgid",
              "2000",
              "egid = file group"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Displayed as s in place of x: -rwsr-xr-x.",
            "Capital S means the bit is set but x is missing.",
            "That combination is almost always a mistake."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why passwd Works"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "-rwsr-xr-x root root /usr/bin/passwd",
            "---------- root root /etc/shadow",
            "# mode 000, yet users change their password"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The controlled program does what you may not.",
            "Others: sudo, su, mount, ping.",
            "ping now often uses cap_net_raw instead."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "setuid, setgid, sticky",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where setuid Is Ignored"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "On shell scripts: every modern Linux kernel drops it.",
            "The #! lookup and the exec are not atomic — a race.",
            "On nosuid mounts: standard for /tmp, /home, USB media.",
            "Use sudoers rules or a small compiled wrapper."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Writing One Safely"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sanitize the environment: PATH, LD_PRELOAD, IFS.",
            "Drop privilege as early as you can.",
            "Validate every path against symlink and TOCTOU races.",
            "setresuid(uid,uid,uid) drops permanently.",
            "Forgetting the saved UID leaves a regain path open."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "setgid On A Directory"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Entirely different meaning: GID inheritance.",
            "New files take the directory's GID, not your primary.",
            "New subdirectories inherit the setgid bit too."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chgrp devs /srv/proj",
            "chmod 2775 /srv/proj   # setgid + rwxrwxr-x",
            "umask 002              # keep group write alive"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Sticky Bit"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Mode 1000, shown as t in the other-x slot.",
            "drwxrwxrwt. A capital T means sticky without x.",
            "Only file owner, dir owner or root may delete or rename.",
            "Even though everyone holds w on the directory."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Path",
            "Mode"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "/tmp",
              "1777"
            ],
            [
              "/var/tmp",
              "1777"
            ],
            [
              "/dev/shm",
              "1777"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "chmod +t dir      # or chmod 1777 dir",
            "chmod -t dir      # clear it",
            "# on executables: obsolete, ignored by Linux"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "chmod -R 777 is not a fix, it is a confession. This handbook walks the whole Linux permission model end to end — the nine bits, why x on a directory means search and not execute, octal by hand, umask as a bit-clear rather than a subtraction, setuid, the sticky bit, POSIX ACLs and their mask trap, and the exact order the kernel checks. Ten pages of dense field notes, including the three questions that diagnose nearly every permission bug.",
